  "concise_summary": "PKD develops a unified theology of justification by grace through faith, arguing that his 2-3-74 meta-abstraction functioned as a divinely-willed satori that instantaneously short-circuited karma and earned justification. He proposes that the fundamental realization was recognizing the Bible not as a book about a world but as the only true reality, while our experienced world is maya (illusion). He then extends this insight to VALIS, discovering that the temporal unity of 2-74 and the spatial unity he perceived in VALIS both express a deeper principle: a unified field of reality encompassing time, space, and causation as apparent rather than ultimate.",
  "key_claims": [
    "The 2-3-74 experience was a satori that revealed the Bible is the only true world and what we perceive is maya, not reality",
    "This meta-abstraction directly caused instantaneous justification, closing the books on karmic debt without further lessons required",
    "God's decision to confer this satori was a free gift of grace, yet mysteriously responsive to PKD's own free-will choice at Covenant House",
    "The unified reality field (VALIS) he perceived is not ultimate reality but a perturbation-detectable interface pointing to an unseen, singular living consciousness (God/Brahman)",
    "Time, space, and causation are all seeming rather than real; they appear to divide unity into multiplicity but all derive from a single common essence",
    "What we experience as plural objects or temporal sequence is actually one thing seen twice (or many times) across spatial and temporal axes"
  ],
